1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,054
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,234 --> 00:00:04,420
Walk past a laundromat and it looks

3
00:00:04,420 --> 00:00:06,855
like the most boring business on earth.

4
00:00:07,035 --> 00:00:08,401
Rows of machines,

5
00:00:08,401 --> 00:00:09,767
a change machine,

6
00:00:09,767 --> 00:00:11,052
a folding table.

7
00:00:11,232 --> 00:00:13,078
But behind that boring front is a

8
00:00:13,078 --> 00:00:15,763
money machine that teaches you almost everything

9
00:00:15,763 --> 00:00:18,280
about how small businesses really make money.

10
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:21,141
Today we're going to open the books,

11
00:00:21,141 --> 00:00:22,110
step by step,

12
00:00:22,110 --> 00:00:23,823
using a simple example.

13
00:00:24,003 --> 00:00:24,860
And by the end,

14
00:00:24,860 --> 00:00:26,516
you'll be able to look at any

15
00:00:26,516 --> 00:00:28,743
boring business and see the math behind

16
00:00:28,743 --> 00:00:28,914
it.

17
00:00:29,264 --> 00:00:30,495
The laundromat math.

18
00:00:30,675 --> 00:00:32,284
Revenue, costs, profit,

19
00:00:32,284 --> 00:00:34,172
what the business is worth,

20
00:00:34,172 --> 00:00:36,619
and how buyers actually pay for it.

21
00:00:36,969 --> 00:00:38,200
Why a laundromat?

22
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:41,246
Because it's simple enough to understand in

23
00:00:41,246 --> 00:00:43,179
one video, and it has all the

24
00:00:43,179 --> 00:00:44,446
pieces that matter:

25
00:00:44,446 --> 00:00:46,779
steady demand, expensive equipment,

26
00:00:46,779 --> 00:00:48,312
a lease, utility bills,

27
00:00:48,312 --> 00:00:49,311
a little labor,

28
00:00:49,311 --> 00:00:51,511
and a sale price based on profit.

29
00:00:51,691 --> 00:00:53,968
Once you understand these pieces,

30
00:00:53,968 --> 00:00:56,314
you can apply the same thinking to

31
00:00:56,314 --> 00:00:58,107
car washes, storage units,

32
00:00:58,107 --> 00:01:01,488
vending routes, cleaning companies and almost any

33
00:01:01,488 --> 00:01:02,523
small business.

34
00:01:02,873 --> 00:01:04,360
One important warning.

35
00:01:04,540 --> 00:01:06,572
The numbers in this video are an

36
00:01:06,572 --> 00:01:09,366
illustration we built to teach the concepts,

37
00:01:09,366 --> 00:01:11,970
not industry averages or a real business.

38
00:01:12,150 --> 00:01:16,054
Real laundromats vary enormously depending on location,

39
00:01:16,054 --> 00:01:18,965
size, prices, competition and management.

40
00:01:19,145 --> 00:01:21,200
Some do much better than our example.

41
00:01:21,380 --> 00:01:22,390
Some lose money.

42
00:01:22,570 --> 00:01:25,091
Always look at real records before making

43
00:01:25,091 --> 00:01:25,890
any decision.

44
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:28,036
We'll go through twelve lessons,

45
00:01:28,036 --> 00:01:29,888
then look at the whole thing from

46
00:01:29,888 --> 00:01:30,617
three angles.

47
00:01:30,797 --> 00:01:32,922
The numbers, meaning the actual math.

48
00:01:33,102 --> 00:01:36,373
The psychology, meaning why people misjudge these

49
00:01:36,373 --> 00:01:37,107
businesses.

50
00:01:37,287 --> 00:01:38,303
And the playbook,

51
00:01:38,303 --> 00:01:40,992
meaning a checklist for evaluating any boring

52
00:01:40,992 --> 00:01:43,203
business before you put a dollar into

53
00:01:43,203 --> 00:01:43,383
it.

54
00:01:43,732 --> 00:01:44,963
Let's open the books.

55
00:01:45,313 --> 00:01:48,646
Our example business is a simplified illustration.

56
00:01:48,826 --> 00:01:50,660
Lending rules come from the U.

57
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:51,200
S.

58
00:01:51,380 --> 00:01:53,361
Small Business Administration,

59
00:01:53,361 --> 00:01:56,003
which describes its seven a loan program

60
00:01:56,003 --> 00:01:58,381
and its requirements on its website.

61
00:01:58,561 --> 00:02:01,466
Business survival data comes from the Bureau

62
00:02:01,466 --> 00:02:02,787
of Labor Statistics.

63
00:02:02,967 --> 00:02:05,936
Market return comparisons use a stated seven

64
00:02:05,936 --> 00:02:09,243
percent average yearly return as an illustration,

65
00:02:09,243 --> 00:02:10,188
not a promise.

66
00:02:10,368 --> 00:02:12,197
This video is education,

67
00:02:12,197 --> 00:02:15,244
not financial, legal or business advice.

68
00:02:15,594 --> 00:02:16,396
Angle one.

69
00:02:16,576 --> 00:02:17,307
The numbers.

70
00:02:17,487 --> 00:02:19,314
Here's our example laundromat,

71
00:02:19,314 --> 00:02:21,872
in a busy neighborhood full of apartments,

72
00:02:21,872 --> 00:02:24,248
where many renters don't have their own

73
00:02:24,248 --> 00:02:25,405
washers and dryers.

74
00:02:25,754 --> 00:02:28,553
Our example laundromat brings in about three

75
00:02:28,553 --> 00:02:30,589
hundred thousand dollars a year:

76
00:02:30,589 --> 00:02:33,388
two hundred twenty thousand from washers and

77
00:02:33,388 --> 00:02:35,996
dryers, sixty thousand from wash and fold

78
00:02:35,996 --> 00:02:38,858
service, and twenty thousand from vending and

79
00:02:38,858 --> 00:02:39,303
extras.

80
00:02:39,653 --> 00:02:40,931
Now the costs.

81
00:02:41,111 --> 00:02:43,512
Rent of about sixty thousand a year,

82
00:02:43,512 --> 00:02:45,779
utilities of about sixty thousand,

83
00:02:45,779 --> 00:02:47,714
labor of forty five thousand,

84
00:02:47,714 --> 00:02:49,581
repairs of fifteen thousand,

85
00:02:49,581 --> 00:02:52,249
and about twenty thousand for insurance,

86
00:02:52,249 --> 00:02:54,183
supplies and everything else.

87
00:02:54,363 --> 00:02:57,103
Total costs: about two hundred thousand.

88
00:02:57,453 --> 00:03:00,454
That leaves about one hundred thousand dollars

89
00:03:00,454 --> 00:03:01,824
a year for the owner,

90
00:03:01,824 --> 00:03:03,194
before loan payments,

91
00:03:03,194 --> 00:03:05,151
depreciation and income taxes.

92
00:03:05,331 --> 00:03:07,181
In the small business world,

93
00:03:07,181 --> 00:03:10,683
this is often called seller's discretionary earnings.

94
00:03:11,033 --> 00:03:13,386
One hundred thousand dollars a year sounds

95
00:03:13,386 --> 00:03:15,178
great, and in our example it is.

96
00:03:15,358 --> 00:03:17,390
But notice how fragile it is.

97
00:03:17,570 --> 00:03:19,634
If revenue falls by ten percent,

98
00:03:19,634 --> 00:03:21,956
that's thirty thousand dollars less,

99
00:03:21,956 --> 00:03:23,955
while most costs stay the same.

100
00:03:24,135 --> 00:03:26,985
Owner earnings drop from one hundred thousand

101
00:03:26,985 --> 00:03:28,251
to seventy thousand,

102
00:03:28,251 --> 00:03:30,277
a thirty percent fall from a ten

103
00:03:30,277 --> 00:03:31,670
percent drop in sales.

104
00:03:31,850 --> 00:03:33,837
That's called operating leverage,

105
00:03:33,837 --> 00:03:35,763
and it works in both directions.

106
00:03:36,112 --> 00:03:37,803
The good news is that it works

107
00:03:37,803 --> 00:03:38,423
upward too.

108
00:03:38,603 --> 00:03:41,118
If the owner adds pickup and delivery

109
00:03:41,118 --> 00:03:43,361
and grows revenue by ten percent,

110
00:03:43,361 --> 00:03:45,060
most costs barely change,

111
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:47,575
so owner earnings could jump by close

112
00:03:47,575 --> 00:03:48,866
to thirty thousand.

113
00:03:49,046 --> 00:03:52,021
That's why the best laundromat owners obsess

114
00:03:52,021 --> 00:03:55,063
over adding revenue without adding much cost:

115
00:03:55,063 --> 00:03:56,010
wash and fold,

116
00:03:56,010 --> 00:03:58,782
commercial accounts like gyms and salons,

117
00:03:58,782 --> 00:04:01,554
longer hours, and card payments that make

118
00:04:01,554 --> 00:04:03,582
customers spend a little more.

119
00:04:03,931 --> 00:04:06,243
Now let's go through the twelve lessons

120
00:04:06,243 --> 00:04:07,902
hidden inside those numbers.

121
00:04:08,252 --> 00:04:09,077
Lesson one.

122
00:04:09,257 --> 00:04:11,393
Demand comes before everything.

123
00:04:11,742 --> 00:04:14,551
A laundromat works because a specific group

124
00:04:14,551 --> 00:04:16,967
of people needs it every single week:

125
00:04:16,967 --> 00:04:18,534
renters without washers,

126
00:04:18,534 --> 00:04:20,885
people in older apartment buildings,

127
00:04:20,885 --> 00:04:22,714
and families with big loads.

128
00:04:22,894 --> 00:04:25,233
Before looking at machines or prices,

129
00:04:25,233 --> 00:04:26,624
the smart question is,

130
00:04:26,624 --> 00:04:28,079
who exactly needs this,

131
00:04:28,079 --> 00:04:30,039
how often, and how many of them

132
00:04:30,039 --> 00:04:31,683
live within a short drive?

133
00:04:32,033 --> 00:04:34,029
That's why location is everything.

134
00:04:34,209 --> 00:04:37,090
A laundromat surrounded by single family homes

135
00:04:37,090 --> 00:04:39,469
with washers and dryers will struggle.

136
00:04:39,649 --> 00:04:41,319
One near dense apartments,

137
00:04:41,319 --> 00:04:43,440
with parking and good visibility,

138
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:45,303
has a built in customer base.

139
00:04:45,483 --> 00:04:48,851
Many experienced buyers look at local population

140
00:04:48,851 --> 00:04:50,956
density, the share of renters,

141
00:04:50,956 --> 00:04:54,184
and the number of competing laundromats within

142
00:04:54,184 --> 00:04:55,026
a few miles.

143
00:04:55,377 --> 00:04:58,018
The same lesson applies to every boring

144
00:04:58,018 --> 00:04:58,627
business.

145
00:04:58,807 --> 00:05:01,972
A septic company needs homes on septic

146
00:05:01,972 --> 00:05:02,639
systems.

147
00:05:02,819 --> 00:05:05,903
A pest control company needs warm weather

148
00:05:05,903 --> 00:05:06,580
and bugs.

149
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:09,752
A storage facility needs people moving in

150
00:05:09,752 --> 00:05:10,336
and out.

151
00:05:10,516 --> 00:05:11,921
Find the demand first,

152
00:05:11,921 --> 00:05:13,070
then the business.

153
00:05:13,250 --> 00:05:14,643
Never the other way around.

154
00:05:14,994 --> 00:05:15,899
Lesson two.

155
00:05:16,079 --> 00:05:17,368
Revenue has layers.

156
00:05:17,718 --> 00:05:18,591
In our example,

157
00:05:18,591 --> 00:05:20,804
self service machines bring in most of

158
00:05:20,804 --> 00:05:21,386
the money.

159
00:05:21,566 --> 00:05:23,979
But the services layered on top,

160
00:05:23,979 --> 00:05:25,412
like wash and fold,

161
00:05:25,412 --> 00:05:26,920
pickup and delivery,

162
00:05:26,920 --> 00:05:29,332
and commercial laundry for gyms,

163
00:05:29,332 --> 00:05:31,142
salons and small hotels,

164
00:05:31,142 --> 00:05:34,384
can grow revenue without needing more floor

165
00:05:34,384 --> 00:05:34,836
space.

166
00:05:35,186 --> 00:05:37,582
Services often earn more per pound of

167
00:05:37,582 --> 00:05:40,366
laundry, and they keep machines busy during

168
00:05:40,366 --> 00:05:41,142
quiet hours.

169
00:05:41,322 --> 00:05:44,305
A laundromat that's packed on Saturday morning

170
00:05:44,305 --> 00:05:46,833
but empty on Tuesday afternoon can fill

171
00:05:46,833 --> 00:05:49,102
those slow hours with wash and fold

172
00:05:49,102 --> 00:05:49,426
work.

173
00:05:49,606 --> 00:05:52,313
That's using the same expensive equipment more

174
00:05:52,313 --> 00:05:53,020
of the time.

175
00:05:53,369 --> 00:05:55,316
The lesson for any business is to

176
00:05:55,316 --> 00:05:57,794
ask: what else could my existing customers

177
00:05:57,794 --> 00:05:59,209
and equipment do for me?

178
00:05:59,389 --> 00:06:01,804
A lawn care company adds snow removal.

179
00:06:01,984 --> 00:06:04,631
A pressure washing company adds gutters.

180
00:06:04,811 --> 00:06:07,319
A cleaning company adds move out cleans.

181
00:06:07,499 --> 00:06:09,755
Layers of revenue on the same base

182
00:06:09,755 --> 00:06:12,674
are how small businesses grow profit without

183
00:06:12,674 --> 00:06:13,536
growing risk.

184
00:06:13,886 --> 00:06:14,908
Lesson three.

185
00:06:15,088 --> 00:06:17,120
The lease is your silent partner.

186
00:06:17,469 --> 00:06:18,514
In our example,

187
00:06:18,514 --> 00:06:21,161
rent is sixty thousand dollars a year,

188
00:06:21,161 --> 00:06:23,182
one of the two biggest costs.

189
00:06:23,362 --> 00:06:25,624
And unlike repairs or supplies,

190
00:06:25,624 --> 00:06:26,427
it's fixed.

191
00:06:26,607 --> 00:06:28,381
It's due whether you have a great

192
00:06:28,381 --> 00:06:29,672
month or a terrible one.

193
00:06:29,852 --> 00:06:31,384
That's why the lease is one of

194
00:06:31,384 --> 00:06:33,479
the most important documents in the whole

195
00:06:33,479 --> 00:06:33,938
business.

196
00:06:34,288 --> 00:06:36,933
Experienced buyers look closely at how many

197
00:06:36,933 --> 00:06:38,531
years remain on the lease,

198
00:06:38,531 --> 00:06:40,683
whether there are options to renew,

199
00:06:40,683 --> 00:06:42,590
and how much rent can increase.

200
00:06:42,770 --> 00:06:45,794
A laundromat with expensive machines and only

201
00:06:45,794 --> 00:06:47,810
two years left on its lease is

202
00:06:47,810 --> 00:06:48,550
a big risk,

203
00:06:48,550 --> 00:06:50,969
because if the landlord won't renew,

204
00:06:50,969 --> 00:06:53,590
moving those machines is costly and you

205
00:06:53,590 --> 00:06:54,867
lose your location.

206
00:06:55,217 --> 00:06:58,064
The lesson: in any location based business,

207
00:06:58,064 --> 00:07:00,116
the lease can matter as much as

208
00:07:00,116 --> 00:07:01,440
the business itself.

209
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:02,865
Read it carefully,

210
00:07:02,865 --> 00:07:04,387
ideally with a lawyer,

211
00:07:04,387 --> 00:07:06,876
and try to negotiate long terms with

212
00:07:06,876 --> 00:07:07,983
renewal options.

213
00:07:08,163 --> 00:07:10,396
A great business in a location you

214
00:07:10,396 --> 00:07:12,563
can lose is not a great business.

215
00:07:12,912 --> 00:07:13,783
Lesson four.

216
00:07:13,963 --> 00:07:16,285
Some costs move with sales.

217
00:07:16,635 --> 00:07:19,352
Utilities are the other big cost in

218
00:07:19,352 --> 00:07:20,827
our example: water,

219
00:07:20,827 --> 00:07:22,302
gas for the dryers,

220
00:07:22,302 --> 00:07:23,543
and electricity.

221
00:07:23,723 --> 00:07:26,494
Unlike rent, utilities rise and fall with

222
00:07:26,494 --> 00:07:28,251
how much the machines run.

223
00:07:28,431 --> 00:07:31,388
More customers means a bigger utility bill,

224
00:07:31,388 --> 00:07:32,901
but also more revenue.

225
00:07:33,251 --> 00:07:36,089
This is the difference between fixed costs

226
00:07:36,089 --> 00:07:37,373
and variable costs.

227
00:07:37,553 --> 00:07:40,280
Fixed costs, like rent and insurance,

228
00:07:40,280 --> 00:07:42,418
stay the same no matter what.

229
00:07:42,598 --> 00:07:45,978
Variable costs, like utilities and supplies,

230
00:07:45,978 --> 00:07:47,207
grow with sales.

231
00:07:47,387 --> 00:07:50,240
A business with mostly fixed costs has

232
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:51,967
big operating leverage:

233
00:07:51,967 --> 00:07:54,295
profits swing a lot with sales.

234
00:07:54,475 --> 00:07:57,267
A business with mostly variable costs is

235
00:07:57,267 --> 00:07:59,989
steadier, but grows profit more slowly.

236
00:08:00,339 --> 00:08:03,287
Owners can lower utility costs with efficient

237
00:08:03,287 --> 00:08:05,973
machines, which use less water and energy

238
00:08:05,973 --> 00:08:06,562
per load.

239
00:08:06,742 --> 00:08:08,389
That's often a big part of the

240
00:08:08,389 --> 00:08:10,640
math when deciding whether to replace old

241
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:11,189
equipment.

242
00:08:11,369 --> 00:08:14,378
Lower variable costs per customer mean more

243
00:08:14,378 --> 00:08:15,848
profit on every load,

244
00:08:15,848 --> 00:08:16,408
forever.

245
00:08:16,757 --> 00:08:17,802
Lesson five.

246
00:08:17,982 --> 00:08:19,332
Equipment wears out,

247
00:08:19,332 --> 00:08:20,885
and that's a real cost.

248
00:08:21,234 --> 00:08:24,742
Commercial washers and dryers are expensive and

249
00:08:24,742 --> 00:08:26,310
they last many years,

250
00:08:26,310 --> 00:08:27,504
but not forever.

251
00:08:27,684 --> 00:08:28,770
In our example,

252
00:08:28,770 --> 00:08:31,377
we listed fifteen thousand a year in

253
00:08:31,377 --> 00:08:31,956
repairs.

254
00:08:32,136 --> 00:08:34,506
But there's a bigger cost that doesn't

255
00:08:34,506 --> 00:08:36,127
show up as a monthly bill:

256
00:08:36,127 --> 00:08:38,434
one day, all those machines will need

257
00:08:38,434 --> 00:08:39,369
to be replaced.

258
00:08:39,719 --> 00:08:42,053
Accountants call this depreciation.

259
00:08:42,233 --> 00:08:44,231
Smart owners think of it as a

260
00:08:44,231 --> 00:08:45,403
replacement fund.

261
00:08:45,583 --> 00:08:47,771
If machines will cost a few hundred

262
00:08:47,771 --> 00:08:50,084
thousand dollars to replace in ten to

263
00:08:50,084 --> 00:08:52,647
fifteen years, part of each year's profit

264
00:08:52,647 --> 00:08:54,836
really belongs to that future bill.

265
00:08:55,016 --> 00:08:57,645
A business that looks profitable but never

266
00:08:57,645 --> 00:09:00,713
saves for equipment replacement is borrowing from

267
00:09:00,713 --> 00:09:01,401
its future.

268
00:09:01,751 --> 00:09:03,511
This is one of the most common

269
00:09:03,511 --> 00:09:06,268
traps when buying any equipment heavy business.

270
00:09:06,448 --> 00:09:08,549
A seller may show strong profits,

271
00:09:08,549 --> 00:09:10,587
but if the machines are near the

272
00:09:10,587 --> 00:09:11,733
end of their life,

273
00:09:11,733 --> 00:09:13,643
the buyer will face a big bill

274
00:09:13,643 --> 00:09:14,853
soon after closing.

275
00:09:15,033 --> 00:09:17,213
Always ask the age of every major

276
00:09:17,213 --> 00:09:18,468
piece of equipment,

277
00:09:18,468 --> 00:09:20,978
and factor replacement into your math.

278
00:09:21,327 --> 00:09:22,361
Lesson six.

279
00:09:22,541 --> 00:09:23,776
Labor is a choice,

280
00:09:23,776 --> 00:09:25,559
and your time has a price.

281
00:09:25,909 --> 00:09:28,553
Many laundromats can run with few employees,

282
00:09:28,553 --> 00:09:30,356
which is part of their appeal.

283
00:09:30,536 --> 00:09:31,620
In our example,

284
00:09:31,620 --> 00:09:34,221
labor is forty five thousand a year,

285
00:09:34,221 --> 00:09:36,822
mostly for wash and fold and keeping

286
00:09:36,822 --> 00:09:37,978
the place clean.

287
00:09:38,158 --> 00:09:40,593
But someone also has to handle repairs,

288
00:09:40,593 --> 00:09:43,278
collections, customer issues and the books.

289
00:09:43,628 --> 00:09:45,312
If the owner does all of that

290
00:09:45,312 --> 00:09:48,156
personally, the business looks more profitable on

291
00:09:48,156 --> 00:09:48,505
paper.

292
00:09:48,685 --> 00:09:50,916
But the owner is really being paid

293
00:09:50,916 --> 00:09:51,901
for their time.

294
00:09:52,081 --> 00:09:53,970
When evaluating a business,

295
00:09:53,970 --> 00:09:56,629
many buyers subtract a fair salary for

296
00:09:56,629 --> 00:09:57,328
a manager.

297
00:09:57,508 --> 00:09:59,843
If the business is still profitable after

298
00:09:59,843 --> 00:10:01,551
paying someone else to run it,

299
00:10:01,551 --> 00:10:02,861
it's a true investment.

300
00:10:03,041 --> 00:10:05,014
If not, it's a job you're buying.

301
00:10:05,364 --> 00:10:07,292
The lesson applies everywhere.

302
00:10:07,472 --> 00:10:09,896
A cleaning company that only makes money

303
00:10:09,896 --> 00:10:12,198
because the owner cleans sixty hours a

304
00:10:12,198 --> 00:10:14,077
week isn't the same as one that

305
00:10:14,077 --> 00:10:15,834
makes money with a team and a

306
00:10:15,834 --> 00:10:16,319
manager.

307
00:10:16,499 --> 00:10:17,769
Both can be good choices,

308
00:10:17,769 --> 00:10:19,598
but you should know which one you're

309
00:10:19,598 --> 00:10:20,005
getting.

310
00:10:20,355 --> 00:10:21,248
Quick pause.

311
00:10:21,428 --> 00:10:23,708
If you're enjoying this kind of business

312
00:10:23,708 --> 00:10:25,759
breakdown, subscribe to the channel.

313
00:10:25,939 --> 00:10:28,374
More of these deep dives are coming,

314
00:10:28,374 --> 00:10:29,321
on car washes,

315
00:10:29,321 --> 00:10:32,298
storage units, vending machines and buying a

316
00:10:32,298 --> 00:10:34,530
business instead of starting one.

317
00:10:34,710 --> 00:10:35,843
Now, lesson seven,

318
00:10:35,843 --> 00:10:37,857
which is where the real money in

319
00:10:37,857 --> 00:10:39,494
boring businesses is made.

320
00:10:39,843 --> 00:10:40,761
Lesson seven.

321
00:10:40,941 --> 00:10:43,333
A business is valued as a multiple

322
00:10:43,333 --> 00:10:44,459
of its earnings.

323
00:10:44,808 --> 00:10:47,188
Small businesses are often priced as a

324
00:10:47,188 --> 00:10:49,255
multiple of their owner earnings.

325
00:10:49,435 --> 00:10:51,481
For a business like our example,

326
00:10:51,481 --> 00:10:54,358
buyers and brokers might talk about something

327
00:10:54,358 --> 00:10:56,532
like three to five times earnings,

328
00:10:56,532 --> 00:10:58,194
depending on the location,

329
00:10:58,194 --> 00:11:00,496
the lease, the age of the equipment,

330
00:11:00,496 --> 00:11:02,797
and how much the business depends on

331
00:11:02,797 --> 00:11:03,437
the owner.

332
00:11:03,617 --> 00:11:05,116
These are rough ranges,

333
00:11:05,116 --> 00:11:07,007
and every deal is negotiated.

334
00:11:07,356 --> 00:11:08,870
So our example laundromat,

335
00:11:08,870 --> 00:11:10,966
earning one hundred thousand a year,

336
00:11:10,966 --> 00:11:13,644
might be valued somewhere around three hundred

337
00:11:13,644 --> 00:11:15,565
to five hundred thousand dollars.

338
00:11:15,745 --> 00:11:17,923
A newer laundromat with a long lease

339
00:11:17,923 --> 00:11:20,041
and modern machines would be at the

340
00:11:20,041 --> 00:11:20,586
high end.

341
00:11:20,766 --> 00:11:22,776
An older one with a short lease

342
00:11:22,776 --> 00:11:25,045
and tired equipment would be at the

343
00:11:25,045 --> 00:11:26,536
low end, or even lower.

344
00:11:26,886 --> 00:11:28,268
Here's why this matters.

345
00:11:28,448 --> 00:11:30,689
Every extra ten thousand dollars of steady

346
00:11:30,689 --> 00:11:32,770
yearly profit could add thirty to fifty

347
00:11:32,770 --> 00:11:34,904
thousand dollars to what the business is

348
00:11:34,904 --> 00:11:35,971
worth when you sell.

349
00:11:36,151 --> 00:11:38,323
That's the hidden engine of wealth in

350
00:11:38,323 --> 00:11:39,379
boring businesses.

351
00:11:39,559 --> 00:11:41,286
You earn the profit every year,

352
00:11:41,286 --> 00:11:43,346
and you build value every time profit

353
00:11:43,346 --> 00:11:43,680
grows.

354
00:11:44,030 --> 00:11:46,530
On screen: owner earnings of one hundred

355
00:11:46,530 --> 00:11:48,030
thousand dollars a year,

356
00:11:48,030 --> 00:11:50,467
valued at three to five times earnings,

357
00:11:50,467 --> 00:11:52,842
gives a value of roughly three hundred

358
00:11:52,842 --> 00:11:55,466
thousand to five hundred thousand dollars.

359
00:11:55,816 --> 00:11:56,734
Lesson eight.

360
00:11:56,914 --> 00:11:58,957
Most buyers don't pay cash.

361
00:11:59,306 --> 00:12:01,828
Many small businesses are bought with a

362
00:12:01,828 --> 00:12:02,151
loan.

363
00:12:02,331 --> 00:12:02,784
The U.

364
00:12:02,964 --> 00:12:03,335
S.

365
00:12:03,515 --> 00:12:07,698
Small Business Administration's seven a program guarantees

366
00:12:07,698 --> 00:12:10,582
loans made by banks to small businesses,

367
00:12:10,582 --> 00:12:13,755
including loans to buy an existing business.

368
00:12:13,935 --> 00:12:16,225
Buyers are usually required to put in

369
00:12:16,225 --> 00:12:17,711
some of their own money,

370
00:12:17,711 --> 00:12:20,125
and lenders look closely at whether the

371
00:12:20,125 --> 00:12:22,538
business's cash flow can cover the loan

372
00:12:22,538 --> 00:12:23,096
payments.

373
00:12:23,445 --> 00:12:25,900
Let's say our buyer pays three hundred

374
00:12:25,900 --> 00:12:28,871
thousand dollars, puts down thirty thousand of

375
00:12:28,871 --> 00:12:29,905
their own money,

376
00:12:29,905 --> 00:12:32,811
and borrows two hundred seventy thousand over

377
00:12:32,811 --> 00:12:35,524
ten years at an illustrative interest rate

378
00:12:35,524 --> 00:12:37,203
of ten and a half percent.

379
00:12:37,383 --> 00:12:39,535
The monthly payment would be about three

380
00:12:39,535 --> 00:12:41,418
thousand six hundred fifty dollars,

381
00:12:41,418 --> 00:12:43,569
or roughly forty four thousand dollars a

382
00:12:43,569 --> 00:12:43,838
year.

383
00:12:44,188 --> 00:12:46,216
So out of one hundred thousand of

384
00:12:46,216 --> 00:12:49,042
owner earnings, about forty four thousand goes

385
00:12:49,042 --> 00:12:50,946
to the loan in the early years,

386
00:12:50,946 --> 00:12:53,465
leaving around fifty six thousand for the

387
00:12:53,465 --> 00:12:56,107
owner before income taxes and the equipment

388
00:12:56,107 --> 00:12:56,414
fund.

389
00:12:56,594 --> 00:12:58,697
Still a solid return on a thirty

390
00:12:58,697 --> 00:13:00,603
thousand dollar down payment,

391
00:13:00,603 --> 00:13:01,852
but with real risk,

392
00:13:01,852 --> 00:13:03,495
because if revenue drops,

393
00:13:03,495 --> 00:13:05,139
the loan payment doesn't.

394
00:13:05,488 --> 00:13:08,485
On screen: two hundred seventy thousand dollars

395
00:13:08,485 --> 00:13:11,162
borrowed over ten years at an illustrative

396
00:13:11,162 --> 00:13:13,330
ten and a half percent costs about

397
00:13:13,330 --> 00:13:15,689
forty four thousand dollars a year in

398
00:13:15,689 --> 00:13:16,263
payments.

399
00:13:16,612 --> 00:13:17,588
Lesson nine.

400
00:13:17,768 --> 00:13:19,600
Debt magnifies results,

401
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:20,635
good and bad.

402
00:13:20,985 --> 00:13:23,071
With thirty thousand dollars down,

403
00:13:23,071 --> 00:13:25,464
our example owner might earn over fifty

404
00:13:25,464 --> 00:13:27,673
thousand a year after loan payments.

405
00:13:27,853 --> 00:13:29,626
That's a huge return on the cash

406
00:13:29,626 --> 00:13:30,291
they put in.

407
00:13:30,471 --> 00:13:32,358
That's the magic of leverage,

408
00:13:32,358 --> 00:13:34,635
and it's why buying a business with

409
00:13:34,635 --> 00:13:36,717
a loan can build wealth so fast.

410
00:13:37,067 --> 00:13:38,797
But leverage cuts both ways.

411
00:13:38,977 --> 00:13:41,644
Remember operating leverage from earlier:

412
00:13:41,644 --> 00:13:43,790
a ten percent drop in revenue cut

413
00:13:43,790 --> 00:13:46,001
owner earnings by thirty thousand.

414
00:13:46,181 --> 00:13:48,372
Now add a fixed loan payment of

415
00:13:48,372 --> 00:13:50,280
forty four thousand on top.

416
00:13:50,460 --> 00:13:52,512
A bad year could leave the owner

417
00:13:52,512 --> 00:13:53,602
with very little,

418
00:13:53,602 --> 00:13:55,525
or even force them to put more

419
00:13:55,525 --> 00:13:56,102
money in.

420
00:13:56,282 --> 00:13:59,072
Two kinds of leverage stacked together create

421
00:13:59,072 --> 00:13:59,754
big swings.

422
00:14:00,103 --> 00:14:02,968
This is why lenders and experienced buyers

423
00:14:02,968 --> 00:14:04,264
look for a cushion:

424
00:14:04,264 --> 00:14:07,469
business earnings well above the loan payments,

425
00:14:07,469 --> 00:14:08,765
plus cash reserves.

426
00:14:08,945 --> 00:14:11,056
Never buy a business where the plan

427
00:14:11,056 --> 00:14:13,229
only works if everything goes right.

428
00:14:13,409 --> 00:14:15,615
Always ask, what happens to me if

429
00:14:15,615 --> 00:14:17,553
revenue drops twenty percent?

430
00:14:17,903 --> 00:14:19,018
Lesson ten.

431
00:14:19,198 --> 00:14:21,566
Always compare to the boring alternative.

432
00:14:21,917 --> 00:14:25,006
Every business decision should be compared with

433
00:14:25,006 --> 00:14:26,649
the simplest alternative:

434
00:14:26,649 --> 00:14:29,345
putting the same money into a diversified

435
00:14:29,345 --> 00:14:31,251
index fund and doing nothing.

436
00:14:31,431 --> 00:14:33,506
At a seven percent illustration,

437
00:14:33,506 --> 00:14:36,619
three hundred thousand dollars would earn around

438
00:14:36,619 --> 00:14:39,278
twenty one thousand dollars in an average

439
00:14:39,278 --> 00:14:40,575
year, with no lease,

440
00:14:40,575 --> 00:14:43,040
no broken machines and no customers to

441
00:14:43,040 --> 00:14:43,494
manage.

442
00:14:43,843 --> 00:14:45,999
So the real question about our laundromat

443
00:14:45,999 --> 00:14:47,524
isn't whether it makes money.

444
00:14:47,704 --> 00:14:50,282
It's whether it makes enough extra money

445
00:14:50,282 --> 00:14:51,572
to justify the risk,

446
00:14:51,572 --> 00:14:53,119
the time and the stress,

447
00:14:53,119 --> 00:14:55,053
compared with the easy option.

448
00:14:55,233 --> 00:14:56,172
For many owners,

449
00:14:56,172 --> 00:14:57,229
the answer is yes,

450
00:14:57,229 --> 00:14:59,636
because they enjoy running a business and

451
00:14:59,636 --> 00:15:01,456
the returns can be much higher.

452
00:15:01,636 --> 00:15:03,739
For others, the index fund is the

453
00:15:03,739 --> 00:15:04,631
better choice.

454
00:15:04,981 --> 00:15:07,606
This comparison protects you from falling in

455
00:15:07,606 --> 00:15:08,859
love with a business.

456
00:15:09,039 --> 00:15:11,299
If the numbers only beat an index

457
00:15:11,299 --> 00:15:12,874
fund by a small amount,

458
00:15:12,874 --> 00:15:15,545
after paying yourself a fair salary for

459
00:15:15,545 --> 00:15:18,558
your time, the business probably isn't worth

460
00:15:18,558 --> 00:15:19,175
the risk.

461
00:15:19,355 --> 00:15:21,081
If they beat it by a lot,

462
00:15:21,081 --> 00:15:21,909
it might be.

463
00:15:22,259 --> 00:15:23,269
Lesson eleven.

464
00:15:23,449 --> 00:15:25,031
Records tell the truth,

465
00:15:25,031 --> 00:15:26,200
and claims don't.

466
00:15:26,551 --> 00:15:28,288
When you look at a business for

467
00:15:28,288 --> 00:15:30,475
sale, the seller will show you numbers.

468
00:15:30,655 --> 00:15:33,043
But numbers on a sales sheet aren't

469
00:15:33,043 --> 00:15:35,362
the same as numbers on tax returns

470
00:15:35,362 --> 00:15:36,727
and bank statements.

471
00:15:36,907 --> 00:15:39,260
In cash heavy businesses especially,

472
00:15:39,260 --> 00:15:42,463
buyers should check that reported revenue matches

473
00:15:42,463 --> 00:15:45,405
deposits, utility bills and machine counters.

474
00:15:45,756 --> 00:15:48,159
Utility bills are a clever check for

475
00:15:48,159 --> 00:15:48,960
laundromats.

476
00:15:49,140 --> 00:15:51,116
Water use is tied to how much

477
00:15:51,116 --> 00:15:52,275
the machines run.

478
00:15:52,455 --> 00:15:54,786
If a seller claims high revenue but

479
00:15:54,786 --> 00:15:56,385
the water bills are low,

480
00:15:56,385 --> 00:15:58,051
something doesn't add up.

481
00:15:58,231 --> 00:16:01,094
Similar checks exist in other businesses:

482
00:16:01,094 --> 00:16:02,840
fuel for delivery routes,

483
00:16:02,840 --> 00:16:05,075
supplies for cleaning companies,

484
00:16:05,075 --> 00:16:07,240
or chemicals for pool services.

485
00:16:07,590 --> 00:16:08,878
The lesson is simple.

486
00:16:09,058 --> 00:16:11,322
Trust, but verify everything.

487
00:16:11,502 --> 00:16:14,281
Use an accountant who knows small business

488
00:16:14,281 --> 00:16:16,662
deals, and walk away from any seller

489
00:16:16,662 --> 00:16:18,515
who won't show real records.

490
00:16:18,695 --> 00:16:21,115
The best deals are often with owners

491
00:16:21,115 --> 00:16:23,199
who kept clean books for years.

492
00:16:23,550 --> 00:16:24,606
Lesson twelve.

493
00:16:24,786 --> 00:16:26,802
The owner's real job is to improve

494
00:16:26,802 --> 00:16:27,572
the business.

495
00:16:27,923 --> 00:16:29,295
Once you own a business,

496
00:16:29,295 --> 00:16:31,754
the biggest wealth comes from improving it.

497
00:16:31,934 --> 00:16:35,343
Adding services, upgrading to efficient machines,

498
00:16:35,343 --> 00:16:37,779
extending hours, improving reviews,

499
00:16:37,779 --> 00:16:39,727
signing commercial accounts,

500
00:16:39,727 --> 00:16:42,441
and building systems so it runs without

501
00:16:42,441 --> 00:16:42,720
you.

502
00:16:42,900 --> 00:16:44,908
Each improvement raises profit,

503
00:16:44,908 --> 00:16:47,306
and every dollar of profit raises the

504
00:16:47,306 --> 00:16:47,694
value.

505
00:16:48,045 --> 00:16:50,636
Imagine our owner grows owner earnings from

506
00:16:50,636 --> 00:16:53,107
one hundred thousand to one hundred forty

507
00:16:53,107 --> 00:16:54,614
thousand over five years,

508
00:16:54,614 --> 00:16:56,241
while paying down the loan.

509
00:16:56,421 --> 00:16:57,755
At a similar multiple,

510
00:16:57,755 --> 00:16:59,937
the business might now be worth well

511
00:16:59,937 --> 00:17:02,059
over four hundred thousand dollars,

512
00:17:02,059 --> 00:17:03,271
with much less debt.

513
00:17:03,451 --> 00:17:05,676
That's how a thirty thousand dollar down

514
00:17:05,676 --> 00:17:07,678
payment can turn into a large amount

515
00:17:07,678 --> 00:17:09,291
of wealth, if things go well.

516
00:17:09,641 --> 00:17:11,583
Then the owner has a choice:

517
00:17:11,583 --> 00:17:13,387
keep it for the cash flow,

518
00:17:13,387 --> 00:17:15,330
or sell it and use the money

519
00:17:15,330 --> 00:17:17,064
to buy a bigger business,

520
00:17:17,064 --> 00:17:18,313
invest, or retire.

521
00:17:18,493 --> 00:17:20,929
Either way, the wealth came from buying

522
00:17:20,929 --> 00:17:22,053
a decent business,

523
00:17:22,053 --> 00:17:23,490
improving it patiently,

524
00:17:23,490 --> 00:17:25,239
and managing risk carefully.

525
00:17:25,589 --> 00:17:26,831
Now here's the payoff.

526
00:17:27,011 --> 00:17:29,535
The same twelve lessons work for almost

527
00:17:29,535 --> 00:17:30,958
every boring business.

528
00:17:31,138 --> 00:17:33,063
Let's apply them quickly to a few

529
00:17:33,063 --> 00:17:33,472
others.

530
00:17:33,822 --> 00:17:34,901
A car wash.

531
00:17:35,081 --> 00:17:37,173
Demand comes from cars in the area

532
00:17:37,173 --> 00:17:38,587
and how dirty they get.

533
00:17:38,767 --> 00:17:40,225
Revenue has layers:

534
00:17:40,225 --> 00:17:42,911
single washes, monthly memberships,

535
00:17:42,911 --> 00:17:43,679
detailing.

536
00:17:43,859 --> 00:17:46,031
Costs include rent or land,

537
00:17:46,031 --> 00:17:49,571
water, chemicals, electricity and equipment.

538
00:17:49,751 --> 00:17:52,595
Equipment is expensive and wears out.

539
00:17:52,775 --> 00:17:55,171
Memberships create recurring revenue,

540
00:17:55,171 --> 00:17:57,373
which buyers usually value highly.

541
00:17:57,553 --> 00:17:59,515
Same math, different machine.

542
00:17:59,865 --> 00:18:01,165
Self storage.

543
00:18:01,345 --> 00:18:03,454
Demand comes from people moving,

544
00:18:03,454 --> 00:18:05,826
downsizing and running out of space.

545
00:18:06,006 --> 00:18:08,224
Revenue is monthly rent per unit.

546
00:18:08,404 --> 00:18:10,140
Costs are mostly fixed:

547
00:18:10,140 --> 00:18:12,102
property taxes, insurance,

548
00:18:12,102 --> 00:18:14,592
maintenance, staff and marketing.

549
00:18:14,772 --> 00:18:17,183
That means huge operating leverage:

550
00:18:17,183 --> 00:18:18,975
once the facility is full,

551
00:18:18,975 --> 00:18:22,144
extra revenue drops almost straight to profit.

552
00:18:22,324 --> 00:18:24,505
But a half empty facility can lose

553
00:18:24,505 --> 00:18:24,890
money.

554
00:18:25,240 --> 00:18:26,261
A vending route.

555
00:18:26,441 --> 00:18:29,556
Demand comes from busy locations with hungry

556
00:18:29,556 --> 00:18:30,052
people.

557
00:18:30,232 --> 00:18:32,368
Revenue is sales per machine.

558
00:18:32,548 --> 00:18:33,793
Costs are products,

559
00:18:33,793 --> 00:18:36,021
commissions to the location owner,

560
00:18:36,021 --> 00:18:38,446
and your time driving and restocking.

561
00:18:38,626 --> 00:18:40,795
Each machine is a tiny business,

562
00:18:40,795 --> 00:18:42,896
and the route is a portfolio of

563
00:18:42,896 --> 00:18:43,235
them.

564
00:18:43,415 --> 00:18:46,231
Efficiency in routing is the difference between

565
00:18:46,231 --> 00:18:47,908
profit and a low paying job.

566
00:18:48,259 --> 00:18:49,454
And a cleaning company.

567
00:18:49,634 --> 00:18:52,943
Demand comes from busy households and offices.

568
00:18:53,123 --> 00:18:55,341
Revenue is recurring contracts.

569
00:18:55,521 --> 00:18:57,218
The biggest cost is labor,

570
00:18:57,218 --> 00:18:58,849
which is mostly variable,

571
00:18:58,849 --> 00:19:01,394
so operating leverage is lower but risk

572
00:19:01,394 --> 00:19:02,243
is lower too.

573
00:19:02,423 --> 00:19:05,252
Value comes from recurring clients and systems

574
00:19:05,252 --> 00:19:06,974
that work without the owner.

575
00:19:07,154 --> 00:19:08,809
Every business is different,

576
00:19:08,809 --> 00:19:11,055
but the questions are always the same.

577
00:19:11,405 --> 00:19:13,812
Let's run our example forward with three

578
00:19:13,812 --> 00:19:16,159
different owners who buy the exact same

579
00:19:16,159 --> 00:19:18,928
laundromat for three hundred thousand dollars.

580
00:19:19,108 --> 00:19:21,193
Same machines, same lease,

581
00:19:21,193 --> 00:19:22,637
same neighborhood.

582
00:19:22,817 --> 00:19:24,686
Made up, but realistic.

583
00:19:25,037 --> 00:19:26,685
Owner one, the improver.

584
00:19:26,865 --> 00:19:29,080
She keeps the place spotless,

585
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:30,989
adds pickup and delivery,

586
00:19:30,989 --> 00:19:33,662
signs contracts with two gyms and a

587
00:19:33,662 --> 00:19:36,641
small hotel, and installs card readers.

588
00:19:36,821 --> 00:19:37,903
Over five years,

589
00:19:37,903 --> 00:19:40,609
revenue grows and owner earnings rise to

590
00:19:40,609 --> 00:19:43,450
around one hundred forty thousand dollars.

591
00:19:43,630 --> 00:19:45,554
She's paid down much of the loan,

592
00:19:45,554 --> 00:19:48,061
and the business is worth considerably more

593
00:19:48,061 --> 00:19:48,878
than she paid.

594
00:19:49,058 --> 00:19:51,599
Her thirty thousand dollar down payment has

595
00:19:51,599 --> 00:19:53,727
turned into a large amount of equity

596
00:19:53,727 --> 00:19:54,909
and a strong income.

597
00:19:55,260 --> 00:19:56,989
Owner two, the absentee.

598
00:19:57,169 --> 00:19:58,923
He thought it was passive income,

599
00:19:58,923 --> 00:20:00,304
so he visits once a month.

600
00:20:00,484 --> 00:20:02,620
Machines stay broken for weeks,

601
00:20:02,620 --> 00:20:04,067
the floor gets dirty,

602
00:20:04,067 --> 00:20:05,720
and online reviews drop.

603
00:20:05,900 --> 00:20:08,922
Customers drift to a competitor two miles

604
00:20:08,922 --> 00:20:09,290
away.

605
00:20:09,470 --> 00:20:11,533
Revenue falls fifteen percent,

606
00:20:11,533 --> 00:20:14,490
which, thanks to operating leverage and the

607
00:20:14,490 --> 00:20:17,172
loan payment, cuts his take home income

608
00:20:17,172 --> 00:20:18,410
by more than half.

609
00:20:18,590 --> 00:20:19,626
Five years later,

610
00:20:19,626 --> 00:20:21,516
he sells for less than he paid.

611
00:20:21,866 --> 00:20:24,002
Owner three, the unlucky one.

612
00:20:24,182 --> 00:20:25,282
He runs it well,

613
00:20:25,282 --> 00:20:26,450
but two years in,

614
00:20:26,450 --> 00:20:29,406
a big new apartment complex nearby includes

615
00:20:29,406 --> 00:20:30,506
in unit washers,

616
00:20:30,506 --> 00:20:33,187
and the landlord raises rent sharply at

617
00:20:33,187 --> 00:20:33,737
renewal.

618
00:20:33,917 --> 00:20:36,703
Revenue falls while costs rise.

619
00:20:36,883 --> 00:20:39,090
He survives because he kept a cash

620
00:20:39,090 --> 00:20:40,843
reserve and didn't overpay,

621
00:20:40,843 --> 00:20:42,595
but his returns are modest.

622
00:20:42,775 --> 00:20:43,746
Not a disaster,

623
00:20:43,746 --> 00:20:45,364
but not the dream either.

624
00:20:45,715 --> 00:20:48,060
Same business, three outcomes.

625
00:20:48,240 --> 00:20:50,715
The improver won because of effort and

626
00:20:50,715 --> 00:20:51,758
smart additions.

627
00:20:51,938 --> 00:20:54,648
The absentee lost because he believed the

628
00:20:54,648 --> 00:20:56,036
passive income story.

629
00:20:56,216 --> 00:20:58,701
And the unlucky owner survived because he

630
00:20:58,701 --> 00:21:00,883
protected himself from the downside.

631
00:21:01,063 --> 00:21:03,680
That's small business in one picture:

632
00:21:03,680 --> 00:21:05,732
effort matters, luck matters,

633
00:21:05,732 --> 00:21:08,844
and risk management decides whether bad luck

634
00:21:08,844 --> 00:21:10,966
is a setback or a catastrophe.

635
00:21:11,317 --> 00:21:13,546
So what does the improver actually do?

636
00:21:13,726 --> 00:21:16,009
Here are five ways to raise profit

637
00:21:16,009 --> 00:21:18,023
in almost any boring business,

638
00:21:18,023 --> 00:21:20,239
without taking on much more risk.

639
00:21:20,589 --> 00:21:21,378
Lever one.

640
00:21:21,558 --> 00:21:23,137
Raise prices carefully.

641
00:21:23,317 --> 00:21:26,494
Many small business owners haven't raised prices

642
00:21:26,494 --> 00:21:27,090
in years.

643
00:21:27,270 --> 00:21:28,569
A modest increase,

644
00:21:28,569 --> 00:21:31,888
explained well, often loses very few customers

645
00:21:31,888 --> 00:21:33,981
and drops straight to profit.

646
00:21:34,161 --> 00:21:35,020
Lever two.

647
00:21:35,200 --> 00:21:37,046
Fill the slow hours.

648
00:21:37,226 --> 00:21:38,983
Discounts for quiet times,

649
00:21:38,983 --> 00:21:41,821
commercial work, or services that use idle

650
00:21:41,821 --> 00:21:44,389
equipment turn dead time into revenue.

651
00:21:44,740 --> 00:21:45,552
Lever three.

652
00:21:45,732 --> 00:21:47,183
Add recurring revenue.

653
00:21:47,363 --> 00:21:52,240
Memberships, subscriptions, maintenance plans and commercial contracts

654
00:21:52,240 --> 00:21:54,260
make income more predictable,

655
00:21:54,260 --> 00:21:56,977
which also raises the business's value.

656
00:21:57,157 --> 00:21:57,958
Lever four.

657
00:21:58,138 --> 00:21:59,868
Cut waste, not quality.

658
00:22:00,048 --> 00:22:03,758
Efficient machines, better energy contracts and smarter

659
00:22:03,758 --> 00:22:07,536
scheduling lower costs without making customers unhappy.

660
00:22:07,886 --> 00:22:08,896
And lever five.

661
00:22:09,076 --> 00:22:10,434
Improve reputation.

662
00:22:10,614 --> 00:22:11,937
In local businesses,

663
00:22:11,937 --> 00:22:14,318
reviews are a form of marketing that

664
00:22:14,318 --> 00:22:14,979
compounds.

665
00:22:15,159 --> 00:22:16,173
A clean, safe,

666
00:22:16,173 --> 00:22:18,998
friendly business with fast fixes and a

667
00:22:18,998 --> 00:22:21,750
responsive owner earns better reviews,

668
00:22:21,750 --> 00:22:23,705
which bring more customers,

669
00:22:23,705 --> 00:22:25,806
which fund more improvements.

670
00:22:25,986 --> 00:22:27,154
Of the five levers,

671
00:22:27,154 --> 00:22:29,244
this one is the cheapest and often

672
00:22:29,244 --> 00:22:30,351
the most powerful.

673
00:22:30,701 --> 00:22:33,204
Finally, the part most people forget to

674
00:22:33,204 --> 00:22:34,102
plan: selling.

675
00:22:34,453 --> 00:22:35,693
When an owner sells,

676
00:22:35,693 --> 00:22:37,802
the buyer looks at the same things

677
00:22:37,802 --> 00:22:39,228
we discussed: earnings,

678
00:22:39,228 --> 00:22:40,531
the lease, equipment,

679
00:22:40,531 --> 00:22:43,074
records and how much the business depends

680
00:22:43,074 --> 00:22:43,880
on the owner.

681
00:22:44,060 --> 00:22:45,851
A business with clean books,

682
00:22:45,851 --> 00:22:46,682
a long lease,

683
00:22:46,682 --> 00:22:49,049
newer machines and a manager in place

684
00:22:49,049 --> 00:22:50,968
can command a higher multiple.

685
00:22:51,148 --> 00:22:53,179
A messy one sells for less,

686
00:22:53,179 --> 00:22:54,909
or doesn't sell at all.

687
00:22:55,260 --> 00:22:57,210
Taxes matter at the exit too.

688
00:22:57,390 --> 00:22:59,680
The sale of a business can create

689
00:22:59,680 --> 00:23:02,110
taxable gains, and how the price is

690
00:23:02,110 --> 00:23:03,776
split between equipment,

691
00:23:03,776 --> 00:23:06,621
goodwill and other assets affects the tax

692
00:23:06,621 --> 00:23:06,968
bill.

693
00:23:07,148 --> 00:23:09,362
That's another reason to work with an

694
00:23:09,362 --> 00:23:11,216
accountant years before a sale,

695
00:23:11,216 --> 00:23:12,233
not weeks before.

696
00:23:12,413 --> 00:23:14,809
Planning ahead can make a meaningful difference

697
00:23:14,809 --> 00:23:15,676
in what you keep.

698
00:23:16,026 --> 00:23:17,632
The best time to prepare for a

699
00:23:17,632 --> 00:23:18,916
sale is the day you buy.

700
00:23:19,096 --> 00:23:20,376
Keep clean records,

701
00:23:20,376 --> 00:23:22,396
document how everything works,

702
00:23:22,396 --> 00:23:25,426
build relationships that don't depend only on

703
00:23:25,426 --> 00:23:27,783
you, and keep the equipment in good

704
00:23:27,783 --> 00:23:28,187
shape.

705
00:23:28,367 --> 00:23:29,792
Even if you never sell,

706
00:23:29,792 --> 00:23:31,899
a business that's ready to sell is

707
00:23:31,899 --> 00:23:33,882
a business that's easier to own.

708
00:23:34,232 --> 00:23:36,252
Before we get into the psychology,

709
00:23:36,252 --> 00:23:37,738
let's protect your money.

710
00:23:37,918 --> 00:23:40,061
Here are ten red flags I'd look

711
00:23:40,061 --> 00:23:42,343
for before buying any laundromat,

712
00:23:42,343 --> 00:23:44,003
car wash, vending route,

713
00:23:44,003 --> 00:23:45,731
or other boring business.

714
00:23:45,911 --> 00:23:48,306
Any one of them isn't automatically a

715
00:23:48,306 --> 00:23:50,895
deal breaker, but each one deserves hard

716
00:23:50,895 --> 00:23:51,542
questions.

717
00:23:51,892 --> 00:23:53,087
Red flag one.

718
00:23:53,267 --> 00:23:55,561
The seller can't or won't show tax

719
00:23:55,561 --> 00:23:56,100
returns.

720
00:23:56,280 --> 00:23:58,418
If the business earns what they say,

721
00:23:58,418 --> 00:24:00,379
the returns should roughly match.

722
00:24:00,559 --> 00:24:03,074
Big gaps usually mean the real numbers

723
00:24:03,074 --> 00:24:05,325
are lower, or the seller is hiding

724
00:24:05,325 --> 00:24:07,840
cash income, which is a problem either

725
00:24:07,840 --> 00:24:08,105
way.

726
00:24:08,285 --> 00:24:09,527
Red flag two.

727
00:24:09,707 --> 00:24:11,016
The lease is short,

728
00:24:11,016 --> 00:24:13,289
or the landlord won't commit to a

729
00:24:13,289 --> 00:24:13,840
renewal.

730
00:24:14,020 --> 00:24:15,150
Without a secure lease,

731
00:24:15,150 --> 00:24:17,017
you're buying a business that could be

732
00:24:17,017 --> 00:24:17,411
evicted.

733
00:24:17,760 --> 00:24:19,119
Red flag three.

734
00:24:19,299 --> 00:24:21,180
The equipment is old and the price

735
00:24:21,180 --> 00:24:22,120
assumes it isn't.

736
00:24:22,300 --> 00:24:24,798
Ask the age of every major machine

737
00:24:24,798 --> 00:24:26,782
and what replacements cost.

738
00:24:26,962 --> 00:24:28,250
Red flag four.

739
00:24:28,430 --> 00:24:30,450
Revenue jumped right before the sale.

740
00:24:30,630 --> 00:24:32,466
Sometimes owners push prices,

741
00:24:32,466 --> 00:24:35,314
cut maintenance, or stop paying themselves to

742
00:24:35,314 --> 00:24:37,213
make the last year look great.

743
00:24:37,393 --> 00:24:39,308
Look at three to five years,

744
00:24:39,308 --> 00:24:39,855
not one.

745
00:24:40,204 --> 00:24:41,331
Red flag five.

746
00:24:41,511 --> 00:24:44,123
One customer or one contract is a

747
00:24:44,123 --> 00:24:45,864
huge share of revenue.

748
00:24:46,044 --> 00:24:47,514
If that customer leaves,

749
00:24:47,514 --> 00:24:49,411
the business changes overnight.

750
00:24:49,591 --> 00:24:50,915
Red flag six.

751
00:24:51,095 --> 00:24:52,871
The owner is the business.

752
00:24:53,051 --> 00:24:54,440
If every relationship,

753
00:24:54,440 --> 00:24:56,966
skill and decision lives in the seller's

754
00:24:56,966 --> 00:24:59,303
head, what you're buying may walk out

755
00:24:59,303 --> 00:25:00,818
the door on closing day.

756
00:25:00,998 --> 00:25:02,754
Ask how long they'll stay to train

757
00:25:02,754 --> 00:25:02,960
you.

758
00:25:03,310 --> 00:25:04,495
Red flag seven.

759
00:25:04,675 --> 00:25:06,497
A new competitor is coming,

760
00:25:06,497 --> 00:25:08,926
or the neighborhood is changing in a

761
00:25:08,926 --> 00:25:10,410
way that hurts demand.

762
00:25:10,590 --> 00:25:11,513
Drive the area,

763
00:25:11,513 --> 00:25:13,114
talk to nearby businesses,

764
00:25:13,114 --> 00:25:15,083
and check city planning records.

765
00:25:15,263 --> 00:25:16,412
Red flag eight.

766
00:25:16,592 --> 00:25:18,349
The seller is in a hurry and

767
00:25:18,349 --> 00:25:20,482
pressures you to skip inspections.

768
00:25:20,662 --> 00:25:23,251
A good deal survives due diligence.

769
00:25:23,431 --> 00:25:25,439
A bad deal needs you to rush.

770
00:25:25,789 --> 00:25:26,939
Red flag nine.

771
00:25:27,119 --> 00:25:28,952
Unpaid bills, liens,

772
00:25:28,952 --> 00:25:30,602
or legal disputes.

773
00:25:30,782 --> 00:25:32,500
A lawyer can search for these,

774
00:25:32,500 --> 00:25:35,076
and the purchase agreement should protect you

775
00:25:35,076 --> 00:25:36,680
from the seller's old debts.

776
00:25:36,860 --> 00:25:38,346
And red flag ten.

777
00:25:38,526 --> 00:25:40,950
The numbers only work if everything goes

778
00:25:40,950 --> 00:25:41,556
perfectly.

779
00:25:41,736 --> 00:25:43,430
If one broken machine,

780
00:25:43,430 --> 00:25:44,816
one rent increase,

781
00:25:44,816 --> 00:25:47,279
or one slow month makes the loan

782
00:25:47,279 --> 00:25:50,513
payment impossible, the deal is too tight.

783
00:25:50,693 --> 00:25:53,642
Walk away, or negotiate a lower price.

784
00:25:53,992 --> 00:25:56,909
Here's a simple rule many experienced buyers

785
00:25:56,909 --> 00:25:59,229
use: assume revenue comes in ten to

786
00:25:59,229 --> 00:26:02,012
fifteen percent lower than the seller says

787
00:26:02,012 --> 00:26:04,465
and costs come in ten percent higher.

788
00:26:04,645 --> 00:26:06,709
If the business still pays the loan,

789
00:26:06,709 --> 00:26:08,830
covers your living costs and leaves a

790
00:26:08,830 --> 00:26:11,181
cushion, you may have a real opportunity.

791
00:26:11,361 --> 00:26:12,261
If it doesn't,

792
00:26:12,261 --> 00:26:14,703
you've just saved yourself from a very

793
00:26:14,703 --> 00:26:15,796
expensive lesson.

794
00:26:16,146 --> 00:26:19,235
Due diligence feels slow and expensive.

795
00:26:19,415 --> 00:26:21,824
A few thousand dollars for an accountant,

796
00:26:21,824 --> 00:26:24,175
a lawyer and an equipment inspection can

797
00:26:24,175 --> 00:26:25,115
feel like a lot.

798
00:26:25,295 --> 00:26:27,535
But compared with a three hundred thousand

799
00:26:27,535 --> 00:26:30,202
dollar mistake, it's the cheapest insurance you'll

800
00:26:30,202 --> 00:26:30,682
ever buy.

801
00:26:31,032 --> 00:26:32,043
Angle two.

802
00:26:32,223 --> 00:26:33,349
The psychology.

803
00:26:33,529 --> 00:26:35,832
Why do smart people make bad decisions

804
00:26:35,832 --> 00:26:37,650
when buying boring businesses?

805
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,299
The first trap is the passive income

806
00:26:40,299 --> 00:26:40,682
dream.

807
00:26:40,862 --> 00:26:44,561
Laundromats, vending and storage are often sold

808
00:26:44,561 --> 00:26:46,528
online as passive income.

809
00:26:46,708 --> 00:26:50,017
People imagine collecting money while doing nothing.

810
00:26:50,197 --> 00:26:53,452
Then they discover broken machines at midnight,

811
00:26:53,452 --> 00:26:56,153
unhappy customers and tricky landlords.

812
00:26:56,333 --> 00:26:59,544
Expecting passive income leads people to underestimate

813
00:26:59,544 --> 00:27:01,328
the work, and to overpay for a

814
00:27:01,328 --> 00:27:03,647
business they don't really want to run.

815
00:27:03,997 --> 00:27:06,586
The second trap is anchoring on revenue.

816
00:27:06,766 --> 00:27:09,619
Three hundred thousand dollars of revenue sounds

817
00:27:09,619 --> 00:27:10,273
impressive.

818
00:27:10,453 --> 00:27:12,113
But revenue isn't profit.

819
00:27:12,293 --> 00:27:14,411
Buyers who focus on the big top

820
00:27:14,411 --> 00:27:17,281
line number often forget to subtract every

821
00:27:17,281 --> 00:27:19,536
cost, a fair salary for their own

822
00:27:19,536 --> 00:27:22,405
time, loan payments and an equipment fund.

823
00:27:22,585 --> 00:27:24,650
Always work down to the money that's

824
00:27:24,650 --> 00:27:25,453
actually left.

825
00:27:25,802 --> 00:27:27,834
The third trap is deal fever.

826
00:27:28,014 --> 00:27:29,529
After months of searching,

827
00:27:29,529 --> 00:27:31,509
buyers want the search to be over.

828
00:27:31,689 --> 00:27:33,543
They start ignoring red flags,

829
00:27:33,543 --> 00:27:35,892
like a short lease or missing records,

830
00:27:35,892 --> 00:27:38,179
because they're emotionally invested.

831
00:27:38,359 --> 00:27:40,813
The best protection is a written checklist

832
00:27:40,813 --> 00:27:41,807
of deal breakers,

833
00:27:41,807 --> 00:27:43,560
made before you start looking,

834
00:27:43,560 --> 00:27:45,371
and a willingness to walk away.

835
00:27:45,721 --> 00:27:48,254
And the fourth trap is overconfidence in

836
00:27:48,254 --> 00:27:49,077
improvements.

837
00:27:49,257 --> 00:27:52,487
Every buyer believes they'll increase revenue and

838
00:27:52,487 --> 00:27:53,146
cut costs.

839
00:27:53,326 --> 00:27:54,092
Some do.

840
00:27:54,272 --> 00:27:56,223
But a price based on the improvements

841
00:27:56,223 --> 00:27:58,121
you plan to make means you're paying

842
00:27:58,121 --> 00:28:00,019
the seller for your own future work.

843
00:28:00,199 --> 00:28:02,643
Smart buyers pay for the business as

844
00:28:02,643 --> 00:28:03,458
it is today,

845
00:28:03,458 --> 00:28:06,105
and keep the upside of improvements for

846
00:28:06,105 --> 00:28:06,852
themselves.

847
00:28:07,201 --> 00:28:08,049
Angle three.

848
00:28:08,229 --> 00:28:08,984
The playbook.

849
00:28:09,164 --> 00:28:11,153
Here's a checklist you can use to

850
00:28:11,153 --> 00:28:12,902
evaluate any boring business.

851
00:28:13,252 --> 00:28:13,450
One.

852
00:28:13,630 --> 00:28:15,298
Demand: who needs this,

853
00:28:15,298 --> 00:28:17,546
how often, and how many of them

854
00:28:17,546 --> 00:28:18,343
are nearby?

855
00:28:18,523 --> 00:28:18,744
Two.

856
00:28:18,924 --> 00:28:20,834
Revenue: what are the layers,

857
00:28:20,834 --> 00:28:22,546
and how much is recurring?

858
00:28:22,726 --> 00:28:23,214
Three.

859
